# Chapter 4, fourth part: the subjects that the fields of the Global Format
# panels are about, each explained where it is set.  These sections belong
# to Global, Format (format3).  Part of the chapter source.

.h3 Justification [format-set-justification]
.ix Justification
.ix Left justification
.ix Right justification
.ix Center justification
.ix Even justification
.ix Ragged right
The justification says how the rows of a block are lined up.
.table 14 14 22
Value | Screen | Printed page
Left | Left | Ragged right
Right | Right | Flush right
Center | Centered | Centered
Even | As Left | Both edges even
.end
It is set for the headlines and for the text (and for each level) on the
Global Format and Level panels, for a block or a column with the {k:F6}
panel ({n:format-local-block}) and for a named format
({n:format-named}).  A new document has Even for its text.  Printed even
justification leaves the last row of a line short; left justification lets
the right edge be a little uneven.

.h3 Indents [format-set-indents]
.ix Indents
.ix Indent
.ix First
.ix All but First
.ix Left block indent
.ix Block Indent
.ix Hanging indent
.ix Ctrl-H
.ix Text indent
.ix Indent per Level
There are three kinds of indent and they are unrelated.
.table 22 14 14
Indent | Screen | Printed page
Indent (lines) | Yes | Yes
Left block indent | Yes | Yes
Indent per Level | Yes | No
.end
{b:Indent} (None, First, All but First, All) says which rows of every line
are indented: none, the first row, every row but the first, or all rows.  The
amount is the first tab stop of the ruler of the block, or 5 cells when it has
no stop.  It is set on the Global Format and Level panels (Headline Indent,
Text Indent), on the Named panel (Indent) and by the {k:F6} question Indent.
A cell is one character of the screen.  In print the unit is a ruler unit
({n:format-fields-spacing}, Ruler): with the default Relative ruler the
default indent of 5 is five eightieths of the width of the text, and at
10/inch it is half an inch.

{b:Left block indent} moves the whole block to the right by that many cells.
It is set by the first {k:F6} question, "L: left block indent (ruler
units)", and by the Block Indent (Left) field of the Named panel.  On the
printed page the text of the block, and the table if it is one, starts that
many ruler units from the left margin.  The block indent is not
checked: a non-numeric entry means 0.

{b:Indent per Level} (Global Format, spacing) indents the blocks of the
sections of each level by that many units more than the level above, and the
{b:Indent to Next Level} of a level panel does the same for the sections below
that level alone: the indent of a section is the sum, over the levels above it,
of the Indent to Next Level of the level, or the Indent per Level where the
level sets none.  The headline and the text of a section are indented
together, and so are its tables, boxes and pictures: a table starts at the
indent of its section and ends at the right margin, so the last column is
the narrower the deeper the section is.  The unit is the unit of the ruler
({n:format-fields-spacing}); the screen draws the indent in cells and the printed page in the unit of the
Ruler field.

{b:Hanging indent.}  Type the characters that hang, such as a bullet and a
tab or a number and a space, and press {k:Ctrl-H}.  The key sets the first tab
stop of the block at the position of the cursor (counted from the block
indent) and the Indent to All but First, as local settings of the block, and
shows "Hanging indent set at 5" (the number is the position).  If the cursor
is still at the left block indent the message is "Type the hanging characters
first, then press {k:Ctrl-H}" and nothing changes.

.h3 Tab stops [format-set-tabs]
.ix Tabs
.ix Tab stops
.ix Ruler, tab stops
.ix Left tab
.ix Right tab
.ix Center tab
.ix Decimal tab
.ix Number tab
The tab stops are a list of positions, in cells, counted from the left block
indent, each with an optional letter for its type.  The list is typed as
text on the Ruler fields of the Global Format and Level panels, the Named
panel and the {k:F6} question Tabs: {c:5,20R,40D}.
.table 8 9 40
Letter | Type | Text is placed
(none), L | Left | Starting at the stop.
R | Right | Ending at the stop.
C | Center | Centered on the stop.
D | Decimal | With its decimal point at the stop.
N | Number | Ending at the stop.
.end
The positions are whole numbers; the stops are used in the order of the list,
so type them in increasing order; 0 is ignored and so is the whole list when
it is 0 or empty (0 in the {k:F6} question means "no tabs"); up to 24 stops
are kept.  The first stop is also the amount of the Indent
({n:format-set-indents}).  A tab pressed past the last stop is one cell wide;
when the list is empty a tab goes to the next multiple of 5.

In the ruler of the screen the stops are marked as shown in
{x:format-screen-ruler}.  On the printed page each piece of text between tabs
is placed at its stop; the stops of the same line are not exact for a
proportional font.  A decimal stop looks for the first period or comma of the
text that follows.

.h3 Section tags [format-set-tags]
.ix Section tags
.ix Section Tag
.ix Auto Numbering
.ix Numbering of sections
A section tag is the number or text that is printed in front of a headline.
By default it is the numbers of the section and of all the sections above it,
joined by points and followed by a blank: 1, 1.1, 1.1.6.  Two settings change
it.  {b:Auto Numbering?} (Global Format, spacing) switches the tags off with
No; the headlines then have no tag, on the screen and in print.
{b:Section Tag} (a field of every Level panel) gives the tag of the headlines
of that level a format of its own.  The format is text in which a code
between backslashes stands for the number of the section among the sections
of its own level (not the numbers above it):
.table 10 50
Code | Number written as
\1\ | Arabic: 1, 2, 3
\i\ | Lower case roman: i, ii, iii
\I\ | Upper case roman: I, II, III
\A\ | Upper case letters: A, B, C ... Z, AA
\a\ | Lower case letters: a, b, c ... z, aa
.end
A roman numeral shows the numbers 1 to 3999: a number above that is written in arabic digits (4000 stays 4000).
Any other character is written as typed, and the blanks at the end of the
text are kept.  With the Level 1 tag "Part \I\. " the first section is "Part
I. Memo", and with the Level 2 tag "(\a\) " its first subsection is "(a) What
changes": the tag of a level replaces the numbers of the levels above, it
does not follow them.  A level that has no Section Tag has the default, and
so do levels 6 and deeper.  The same tags are on the screen and in print.
The tag belongs only to a headline in a structured document and never to the
headline of the root section, the title.  {x:markers} describes the
Cross-reference marker, which prints the tag of a section.

.h3 Spacing [format-set-spacing]
.ix Spacing
.ix Line spacing
.ix Space between blocks
.ix Space between sections
.ix Whitespace in print
.ix Single spacing
.ix Draft and final spacing
Spacing is counted in lines: 1.00 is one line, 1.50 is a line and a half.  It
is set globally (Global Format, spacing) and for a level.  The screen does
not draw any of it.  The printed page uses the five fields of the Format
panels, in a final print only (a draft print, Print Draft, sets everything to
1.00):
.table 19 41
Field | On the printed page
Line Spacing | The lines of the block are stretched to that many lines.
Headline Spacing | Space after a headline.
Block Spacing | Space after a block or table.
Subsection Spacing | Space before the first subsection headline.
Peer Section Spacing | Space before the other headlines.
.end
For the four spaces the amount added is the value minus 1.00 of a line, and a
value of 1.00 or less adds nothing: 1.50 puts half a line of space, 2.00 a
whole line, 0.50 no space.  The line spacing is a stretch of the line distance
of the block: 2.00 doubles it.  A level's own values win over the global ones,
for the headline of that level (Headline, Subsection and Peer Section
Spacing) and for the blocks that are in a section of that level (Line and
Block Spacing).  Subsection Spacing applies to the first headline after a
group of subsections opens, Peer Section Spacing to every headline that
follows another one of the same group.

A block can have a line spacing and a block spacing of its own, through a
named format ({n:format-named}): the fields Line Spacing and Block Spacing of
the Named Block Format panel are numbers of lines in the same way, and they
win over the values of the level and of the document for the blocks that have
that format.  A headline is not a block of that kind: it keeps the spacings
of its level.  As for the others, a draft print sets the lines to one line
and adds no space, and Print Double Spaced doubles the line spacing that the
block has.  A page that a headline begins ({n:format-fields-spacing}) has no
space at its top.
